      "text": "To determine which functions have inverses, we need to check if each function is one-to-one. A function has an inverse if and only if it is a one-to-one function, meaning that each output value corresponds to exactly one input value. In other words, a function is one-to-one if and only if it is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing over its entire domain.\n\nLet's analyze each graph:\n\n1. **Graph A**: There are two distinct horizontal asymptotes, y = 1 and y = -2. This means the function is not one-to-one because it fails the horizontal line test. It is not an invertible function.\n\n2. **Graph B**: The function is a parabola that opens upwards, with a minimum point at (-2, -3). It is a one-to-one function because it passes the horizontal line test and is strictly increasing. Therefore, it has an inverse.\n\n3. **Graph C**: The function is a combination of a cubic function and a circle centered at the origin. The cubic function is strictly increasing, and the circle does not affect the injectivity of the function. Therefore, it is a one-to-one function and has an inverse.\n\n4. **Graph D**: The function is a semicircle that stretches along the y-axis, starting at (0, 2) and ending at (4, 2). It is a one-to-one function because it passes the horizontal line test and is strictly increasing. Therefore, it has an inverse.\n\n5. **Graph E**: The function is a cubic function that passes through the points (0, 2) and (4, 2). The function is not one-to-one because it fails the horizontal line test. Therefore, it does not have an inverse.\n\nBased on the analysis, the functions that have inverses are Graph B, Graph C, and Graph D. Therefore, the answer is:\n\n\\boxed{B, C, D}",
